  <article aria-labelledby="paper-title">
    <h1 id="paper-title">Polynomial compatibility on the Boolean cube from disjoint-support separators</h1>
    <p class="entry-meta"><a href="https://orcid.org/0009-0001-9320-500X">Alec Kriebel</a> · <time datetime="2026-10-17">17 October 2026</time> · Zenodo version 1.0.0 · Unrefereed preprint</p>
    <p>Two low-degree polynomials can vanish at many vertices of a Boolean cube. This note gives an explicit condition ensuring that there is a vertex where <em>both</em> are nonzero: no individual coordinate may account for too much of either polynomial’s full squared norm. The argument is a credited deduction from established decision-tree and covariance results.</p>

    <section id="manuscript" aria-labelledby="manuscript-heading">
      <h2 id="manuscript-heading">Read the manuscript</h2>
      <p><a href="polynomial-compatibility.pdf">Open the original PDF</a> · <a href="polynomial-compatibility.pdf" download>Download PDF</a> · <a href="citation.bib" download>BibTeX</a> · <a href="https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.23443301">Zenodo record and DOI</a></p>
      <iframe class="paper-pdf" src="polynomial-compatibility.pdf#view=FitH" title="Original four-page manuscript: Polynomial compatibility on the Boolean cube from disjoint-support separators"></iframe>
      <p>The original PDF is reproduced unchanged. If the embedded reader is unavailable, use the open or download link. Its printed date is 17 October 2026; it prints no numeric version. Version 1.0.0 belongs to the Zenodo metadata.</p>
    </section>

    <section id="abstract" aria-labelledby="abstract-heading">
      <h2 id="abstract-heading">Original abstract</h2>
      <p id="original-abstract">We give an explicit affirmative answer to the two-polynomial question posed by Austrin in Oberwolfach Report 9/2025. Two nonzero real multilinear polynomials of degree at most d on the uniform Boolean cube have a common nonzero vertex whenever every relative coordinate influence is at most 1/(4096d⁸). In fact, disjoint supports force a relative influence at least 1/(1024d⁸). The result is a deduction from the disjoint-support separator of Li–Li–Li–Liu and the covariance inequality of O’Donnell–Saks–Schramm–Servedio, combined with a square-density estimate. We supply the complete deduction and an explicit separator constant. No novelty or first-priority claim is made for this consequence.</p>
    </section>

    <section id="result" aria-labelledby="result-heading">
      <h2 id="result-heading">The precise guarantee</h2>
      <p>Let <var>n</var>, <var>d</var> ≥ 1 be integers. Let <var>f</var> and <var>g</var> be nonzero real multilinear polynomials on the uniform sign cube {−1, 1}<sup>n</sup>, each of degree at most <var>d</var>. The coordinates are independent fair signs.</p>
      <p>For the multilinear expansion <var>a</var>(<var>x</var>) = ∑<sub>S ⊆ [n]</sub> â(<var>S</var>) ∏<sub>i ∈ S</sub> <var>x</var><sub>i</sub>, define</p>
      <p class="equation">‖<var>a</var>‖<sub>2</sub><sup>2</sup> = E[<var>a</var>(<var>X</var>)<sup>2</sup>] = ∑<sub>S</sub> â(<var>S</var>)<sup>2</sup>,<br>
      RelInf<sub>i</sub>(<var>a</var>) = (∑<sub>S ∋ i</sub> â(<var>S</var>)<sup>2</sup>) / ‖<var>a</var>‖<sub>2</sub><sup>2</sup>.</p>
      <p>The denominator includes the constant Fourier coefficient. It is the full L<sup>2</sup> norm, not the variance. Set δ = max<sub>i</sub> max{RelInf<sub>i</sub>(<var>f</var>), RelInf<sub>i</sub>(<var>g</var>)}.</p>
      <p><strong>Necessary for disjoint supports:</strong> if <var>f</var>(<var>x</var>)<var>g</var>(<var>x</var>) = 0 at every vertex, then δ ≥ 1/(1024<var>d</var><sup>8</sup>).</p>
      <p><strong>Sufficient for compatibility:</strong> if every relative coordinate influence of <em>both</em> polynomials is at most 1/(4096<var>d</var><sup>8</sup>), then some vertex satisfies <var>f</var>(<var>x</var>)<var>g</var>(<var>x</var>) ≠ 0. Both polynomial values are nonzero there; this is not a common-root assertion.</p>
      <p>The supports need not cover the cube: arbitrary common zeros are allowed. Nonzero constants and <var>n</var> &lt; <var>d</var> cause no exception. The zero polynomial is excluded because its relative influence has a zero denominator. Neither the exponent nor the constants are asserted to be optimal.</p>
    </section>

    <section id="argument" aria-labelledby="argument-heading">
      <h2 id="argument-heading">Why the bound follows</h2>
      <p>Assume the two supports are disjoint. The prior separator of <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.03824v1">Li, Li, Li and Liu, Lemma 3.5</a>, supplies a total deterministic decision tree whose output <var>h</var> is 1 on the support of <var>f</var> and 0 on the support of <var>g</var>. Common-zero vertices receive labels too. The note extracts the depth bound <var>T</var> ≤ 16<var>d</var><sup>4</sup> using the classical Markov/Bernoulli argument presented by <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2601.08727v3">Kothari and coauthors</a>. Queries do not repeat along a branch.</p>
      <p>Replace each polynomial by its normalized squared density: <var>u</var> = <var>f</var><sup>2</sup>/‖<var>f</var>‖<sub>2</sub><sup>2</sup> and <var>v</var> = <var>g</var><sup>2</sup>/‖<var>g</var>‖<sub>2</sub><sup>2</sup>. Both have expectation 1, while <var>hu</var> = <var>u</var> and <var>hv</var> = 0. Hence |Cov(<var>h</var>, <var>u</var>)| + |Cov(<var>h</var>, <var>v</var>)| = 1.</p>
      <p>The real covariance inequality of <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/cs/0508071v1">O’Donnell, Saks, Schramm and Servedio, §3.3, Eq. (6)</a> gives |Cov(<var>h</var>, <var>w</var>)| ≤ ½ ∑<sub>i</sub> <var>r</var><sub>i</sub> A<sub>i</sub>(<var>w</var>), where <var>r</var><sub>i</sub> is the probability of querying coordinate <var>i</var>, and A<sub>i</sub>(<var>w</var>) = E|<var>w</var>(<var>X</var>) − <var>w</var>(<var>X</var><sup>(i)</sup>)|. Here <var>X</var><sup>(i)</sup> independently <em>resamples</em> that coordinate, leaving it unchanged with probability one-half. The inequality permits adaptive queries and variable branch lengths; <var>w</var> need not be computed by the tree.</p>
      <p>Writing <var>a</var> = <var>b</var> + <var>x</var><sub>i</sub><var>c</var> and applying Cauchy–Schwarz gives the square-density estimate A<sub>i</sub>(<var>a</var><sup>2</sup>/‖<var>a</var>‖<sub>2</sub><sup>2</sup>) ≤ 2√(ρ<sub>i</sub>(1 − ρ<sub>i</sub>)) ≤ 2√ρ<sub>i</sub>, with ρ<sub>i</sub> = RelInf<sub>i</sub>(<var>a</var>). Combining these statements and ∑<sub>i</sub> <var>r</var><sub>i</sub> ≤ <var>T</var> yields</p>
      <p class="equation">1 ≤ 2<var>T</var>√δ ≤ 32<var>d</var><sup>4</sup>√δ.</p>
      <p>Squaring gives the necessary 1/(1024<var>d</var><sup>8</sup>) bound. At δ ≤ 1/(4096<var>d</var><sup>8</sup>), the last expression is at most ½, a contradiction. The written proof treats the total tree and adaptive resampling directly.</p>
    </section>

    <section id="context" aria-labelledby="context-heading">
      <h2 id="context-heading">Question, credit and scope</h2>
      <p>The target is Per Austrin’s two-polynomial question in <a href="https://doi.org/10.4171/OWR/2025/9">Oberwolfach Report 9/2025, printed page 445</a>. The note supplies the explicit application, square-density conversion and extracted separator constant. The separator, real covariance inequality and Markov/Bernoulli method are prior inputs. No novelty or first-priority claim is made for this consequence.</p>
      <p>The earlier sufficient threshold from the CRYPTO 2022 work is strict: δ &lt; 2<sup>−d</sup>/<var>d</var>. The non-strict endpoint printed in the workshop report fails already at <var>d</var> = 1 for <var>f</var> = 1 + <var>x</var><sub>1</sub> and <var>g</var> = 1 − <var>x</var><sub>1</sub>, each with relative influence ½. The present inclusive 1/(4096<var>d</var><sup>8</sup>) threshold has slack.</p>
      <p>This theorem concerns exactly two real multilinear polynomials on the uniform Boolean cube. It does not settle the general distribution formulations in the CRYPTO paper, other groups or measures, or approximate-support variants. The proof gives no polynomial-runtime procedure for finding the vertex.</p>
      <p>Historical comparison remains bounded by the versions inspected. The original note records that the full ePrint 2022/218 and full 2023/570 follow-up were outside its accessible full-body set; the official CRYPTO 2022 conference version was treated separately. This does not establish equivalence of editions, exhaustive coverage, firstness or the current global literature status.</p>
    </section>

